WEBVTT

00:00:00.534 --> 00:00:21.054
<v Casey>Hey there — today's a big one. Two former Williams Lake mayors squaring off on the same ballot, a councillor's public temper tantrum that might tell you more than his budget numbers, and three regional director seats we straight up can't name a single candidate for. Plus, who decides what a council term was actually worth once the new person takes the chair. Coming right up.

00:00:34.529 --> 00:00:54.873
<v Casey>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities — I'm Casey. It's Saturday, September 19th, 2026. Coming to you from the Cariboo, on lands that have been cared for by the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh peoples since time immemorial. Today's theme is Cariboo Local Affairs.

00:00:55.646 --> 00:01:28.998
<v Riley>And I'm Riley. With the election five weeks out, today's about who's actually running to make the decisions in your town — mayors, councils, school trustees, and the regional director seats most people forget exist. Quick weather check first. Out at Horsefly Lake this morning it's overcast, around ten degrees, high of twenty-three, low of eight, no precipitation expected. 100 Mile House is sitting around ten with cloud breaking up through the day, Williams Lake's at nine and overcast, Quesnel's ten and partly cloudy, and Redstone's the coolest of the bunch at eight.

00:01:29.676 --> 00:01:34.780
<v Casey>Good window for door-knocking if you're one of the candidates we're about to talk about. Let's get into it.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:43.028
<v Casey>Six stories close to home to start, one that ties straight into today's theme, and a longer tail after that. This is a full one.

00:01:44.366 --> 00:02:17.414
<v Riley>Williams Lake Legion Branch 139 cut the ribbon on a new veterans crosswalk this week. Members, veterans, firefighters, and RCMP walked it together at Friday's opening. It's a modest piece of infrastructure — paint and signage, essentially — but it exists because a legion branch pushed a small, specific ask through the city rather than waiting for someone else to prioritize it. Somebody approved a work order, a crew painted a crosswalk, and now there's a safer way for veterans to cross a street on Remembrance Day. Nobody ran a campaign on it.

00:02:17.969 --> 00:02:19.969
<v Casey>That's a council afternoon well spent.

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:42.178
<v Casey>The Tribune also carried a harder story out of the Okanagan. A municipal councillor there was found guilty in what the court described as using his position as a youth coach to find boys for what the ruling called companionship, and he's been removed from his council seat as a result. We're not naming him further — the finding and the removal are the facts that matter.

00:02:43.636 --> 00:02:48.812
<v Riley>"Local government" isn't automatically low-stakes just because the population number is small.

00:02:50.089 --> 00:03:23.089
<v Riley>The B.C. Rail Corridor Alliance is still pressing the province for funding and for governance authority over that stretch of rail line. We flagged this on Wednesday, when 100 Mile House Mayor Maureen Pinkney spoke at the Union of BC Municipalities about it. Nothing's moved since — Victoria hasn't answered on the dollars or on who would hold operational control if the corridor changes hands. The alliance exists precisely because no single municipality has the standing to make that request alone, and collectively they're still waiting.

00:03:23.618 --> 00:03:26.274
<v Casey>So the ask is the same ask. It's just older now.

00:03:27.864 --> 00:03:54.200
<v Casey>Highway 97 south of Quesnel is back open to two-lane traffic after RCMP, BC Ambulance, and the Quesnel Fire Department responded to a two-vehicle collision near Quartz Road Friday afternoon, just after three-thirty. There's ongoing construction in that stretch — the southbound lane closes intermittently and the limit through the zone drops to fifty. Two-way traffic is maintained throughout, so no major delays, but ease off if you're driving that corridor this weekend.

00:03:55.577 --> 00:04:17.489
<v Riley>Two court updates out of Quesnel, both procedural. A twenty-five-year-old man charged in connection with a large drug seizure in Quesnel now has a trial date, and Paul Plummer, fifty-three, accused of discharging a firearm inside a residence in the Quesnel area, has been ordered to stand trial — his date is March 9th, 2027, more than a year out.

00:04:18.613 --> 00:04:25.821
<v Casey>That gap between charge and trial is the actual pace of the system out here, and it's why rural crime coverage so often feels unresolved.

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:49.192
<v Riley>On decisions that get no attention but shape how an operation runs — Hobby Farms published a comparison of square versus round hay balers for small operations, weighing storage space, equipment cost, and herd size. Mundane on its face, but the logic is the logic a council uses at a bigger scale: what fits your actual capacity, not what looks impressive on paper.

00:04:50.633 --> 00:04:56.497
<v Casey>Every small farm is basically a one-person municipal government with better fences and worse public meetings.

00:04:58.164 --> 00:05:14.636
<v Casey>From further afield, ScienceDaily reported researchers have identified a population of cells that can start the process of programmed cell death, survive it, and then help rebuild the damaged tissue around them — their descendants apparently come back more resistant to future damage than the originals were.

00:05:15.283 --> 00:05:18.539
<v Riley>Cellular near-death experience with a résumé boost.

00:05:20.131 --> 00:05:32.227
<v Riley>STAT News covered how Sudan's civil war has made it dangerous, sometimes impossible, for cancer patients and people with other long-term illnesses to reach treatment that used to be routinely available.

00:05:33.450 --> 00:05:47.930
<v Casey>ScienceAlert has a piece on a tiny freshwater animal, the bdelloid rotifer, that researchers say can reproduce without ever putting its shattered chromosomes back together — something they say hasn't been documented in any other living organism, tardigrades included.

00:05:48.464 --> 00:05:52.664
<v Riley>Nature keeps finding stranger ways to survive damage that should be fatal.

00:05:54.256 --> 00:06:12.216
<v Riley>Tom's Hardware has the story of a crypto ringleader — his gang reportedly started as meetups on Minecraft servers — pleading guilty to a racketeering charge tied to a two-hundred-and-forty-five-million-dollar heist. Supercars, private jets, a personal security detail, and up to twenty years on the table.

00:06:12.755 --> 00:06:16.747
<v Casey>From a Minecraft lobby to federal sentencing guidelines is quite the career arc.

00:06:18.450 --> 00:06:32.626
<v Casey>APTN reported that a citizen of the Vuntut Gwitchin First Nation has filed a petition with the Yukon Supreme Court asking to delay an upcoming election for chief and council, arguing the results might not hold up legally once the court reviews the process.

00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:48.950
<v Riley>The New York Times ran a piece asking why European cities that publicly complain about too many tourists — Venice, Barcelona, Mallorca among them — keep expanding their airports anyway, which undercuts the restraint policies those same cities say they want.

00:06:50.163 --> 00:07:03.227
<v Casey>Governance contradictions aren't only a small-town problem. The Atlantic also had a piece on free-speech pressures tied to the current U.S. administration — we'll leave it there rather than get into American politics on a Cariboo show.

00:07:04.454 --> 00:07:16.582
<v Riley>One more to close out. Tom's Guide tested a tool called SkillSync that transfers your ChatGPT conversation history over to Claude without starting from scratch — months of built-up context moved in one pass.

00:07:17.856 --> 00:07:28.312
<v Casey>We use tools from both of those companies to put this show together, for what it's worth. Staying on the theme of work that happens without anyone announcing it, let's do today's spotlight.

00:07:29.712 --> 00:07:54.528
<v Casey>Today's spotlight goes to the 100 Mile House Food Bank Society. It's volunteer-run — about twenty people, no paid staff — putting together weekly hampers and getting perishable food out to families across the South Cariboo. No head office, no marketing budget, just neighbours making sure other neighbours eat through the winter. If you'd like to donate or put your name down to volunteer, they're at 100milehousefoodbank.ca.

00:07:55.121 --> 00:08:04.601
<v Riley>Twenty people keeping that going week after week is the kind of infrastructure nobody puts on a campaign sign, and it's exactly what holds a town together in February.

00:08:05.254 --> 00:08:09.518
<v Casey>Speaking of what does and doesn't make it onto a campaign sign — into the Deep Dive.

00:08:10.918 --> 00:08:30.950
<v Riley>Nominations closed earlier this month. Five weeks left before the vote on October 17th. And sitting underneath all the candidate counts is a question this ballot actually forces — when a council term ends, who decides which parts of it were worth keeping, and which parts get erased the moment someone new takes the chair?

00:08:31.292 --> 00:08:37.388
<v Casey>Because voters don't get to keep the parts they liked and toss the rest. They get a whole person, record and all.

00:08:37.914 --> 00:08:46.522
<v Riley>Which makes Williams Lake the clearest test case this year. Two names for mayor, fifteen for council. One of the mayoral candidates is Walt Cobb.

00:08:47.175 --> 00:09:02.103
<v Casey>Cobb's not new to this. Mayor from 1990 to 1996. Mayor again from 2014 to 2022. Now he's back looking for a fourth term. Two former mayors on one ballot — a sequel nobody pitched, and the whole town's going to watch it anyway.

00:09:02.649 --> 00:09:24.817
<v Riley>And the man running against him is the man who beat him last time. Surinderpal Rathor took the seat from Cobb in October 2022 with roughly thirty-four percent of the vote in a four-candidate field — somewhere in the range of eleven hundred votes. Jason Ryll, a councillor at the time, came second with about twenty-eight percent. Cobb finished behind that.

00:09:25.419 --> 00:09:38.723
<v Casey>Thirty-four percent in a four-way split is a plurality, not a mandate. Two-thirds of the people who voted in 2022 picked someone else, which is the opening Cobb is running at — a straight two-way race changes that arithmetic completely.

00:09:39.376 --> 00:09:54.112
<v Riley>It also raises the stakes on Rathor's record, because a two-way race means the record is the ballot question. When he announced in June, he pointed at cutting the city's debt by four million dollars and reducing the 2025 mill rate.

00:09:54.651 --> 00:10:10.531
<v Casey>Checkable claims, and they should get tested at an all-candidates forum rather than repeated from a press release. Debt reduction alone doesn't tell you whether services held while it happened, or whether it came out of deferred maintenance that lands as a bill in 2028.

00:10:11.127 --> 00:10:29.367
<v Riley>Probe it, absolutely — but the deferred-maintenance theory runs into something from this past spring. Rathor was publicly pressing council and Victoria on stalled local infrastructure projects, which is the opposite behaviour from a mayor quietly letting capital work rot to make a debt number look good.

00:10:29.988 --> 00:10:40.756
<v Casey>Pressing Victoria is free. Funding the work yourself isn't. A mayor can do both — trim locally and lobby upward for someone else's money — and voters can't tell those apart from a mill rate alone.

00:10:41.289 --> 00:10:59.281
<v Riley>Which is why the rest of the record matters too, including the less flattering parts. A formal complaint of conflict of interest was filed against Rathor while he was still a councillor. Council held an in-camera meeting, passed a resolution letting him respond publicly, and he denied any conflict.

00:10:59.794 --> 00:11:14.458
<v Casey>And there's an older item that's murkier. Rathor was arrested and charged with obstruction of justice around 2012, tied to volunteer work he did as an RCMP translator on a Christmas Eve assault case. He told reporters at the time he was still in shock.

00:11:15.033 --> 00:11:30.369
<v Riley>We couldn't find a resolution to that charge anywhere in the sources we checked — no trial record, no stay, no acquittal. That stays an open item. It's an allegation from more than a decade ago, not a finding, and reporting it as anything more would be wrong.

00:11:30.945 --> 00:11:51.257
<v Casey>Agreed, and it's the least useful thing on the list for a 2026 voter anyway. The relevant material is recent: this past May, Rathor and Councillor Scott Nelson got into a heated public exchange over a proposed increase to the chamber of commerce budget, and Rathor lost his temper on the record. That's not history. That's how he chairs a meeting right now.

00:11:51.884 --> 00:12:04.628
<v Riley>Temper in a council chamber is also how you find out what a mayor actually cares about. I'd rather have a mayor who argues in public than one who settles it in the parking lot afterward and brings a unanimous vote to the room.

00:12:05.196 --> 00:12:18.948
<v Casey>Except the parking-lot version at least gets the budget passed. Shouting matches burn staff time and make councillors reluctant to bring anything contentious forward — the cost shows up as things that never reach the agenda, which no voter ever sees.

00:12:19.530 --> 00:12:28.378
<v Riley>So the choice is between institutional memory voters already rejected once, and a term with a specific fiscal record attached to a specific governing style.

00:12:28.951 --> 00:12:41.167
<v Casey>Quesnel doesn't have that tension built in at all. Three candidates for mayor, eighteen for council, and the Observer's framing of the nomination period was that every sitting incumbent — mayor included — filed to run again.

00:12:41.688 --> 00:12:58.840
<v Riley>Ron Paull is that incumbent. He beat Bob Simpson in 2022, and the Observer called it his "fourth time lucky," meaning Paull had run for that chair at least three times before and lost. That's not a safe seat handed over — that's a guy who kept showing up until the town agreed.

00:12:59.518 --> 00:13:15.798
<v Casey>He also won a race where he and Simpson took more than ninety percent of the vote between them. Two real options, a clear verdict. Compare that to a ballot with eighteen council candidates and no serious challenge at the top, and voters have to work to find where the actual disagreements are.

00:13:16.466 --> 00:13:31.138
<v Riley>Eighteen candidates for council is the disagreement. That's the seat where policy gets argued out in this region, and a crowded council field with a stable mayor can produce more scrutiny than a dramatic mayoral fight that eats all the oxygen.

00:13:31.722 --> 00:13:40.826
<v Casey>Only if anyone's covering the council race. Crowded fields with no headline fight are exactly the ones that get one nomination-day article and then silence until voting day.

00:13:41.354 --> 00:13:55.770
<v Riley>100 Mile House sits between the two. Two mayoral candidates, ten for council. And the mayoral race there carries an asterisk — Maureen Pinkney took the chair in 2022 by acclamation, after Mitch Campsall chose not to run again.

00:13:56.064 --> 00:13:59.272
<v Casey>Meaning she's never had to win a contested vote for that seat.

00:13:59.812 --> 00:14:15.620
<v Riley>And we should be straight that our source material doesn't confirm by name whether Pinkney is one of the two 2026 mayoral candidates. The coverage gives us a count, not names. If she is running, this October would be her first contested test as mayor.

00:14:16.258 --> 00:14:27.986
<v Casey>That's a materially different claim to put in front of voters than what Cobb and Rathor are both carrying, where there's an electoral record on both sides. Someone at the paper should confirm it plainly before the forums start.

00:14:28.539 --> 00:14:38.203
<v Riley>A fourth ballot in this election gets almost no attention at all — School District 27 trustee. Fourteen candidates filed across the whole Cariboo-Chilcotin.

00:14:38.834 --> 00:14:56.498
<v Casey>And that board's already had turnover mid-term. A trustee by-election was scheduled for the district, got cancelled, and was rescheduled before it finally went ahead in 2025, with Mark Walsh serving as chief election officer. Process hiccups like that on a small rural board are their own quiet signal.

00:14:57.061 --> 00:15:06.645
<v Riley>Which means whoever won that by-election has been sitting as a trustee for about a year and is presumably one of these fourteen names, asking voters for a full term.

00:15:07.221 --> 00:15:21.021
<v Casey>And nothing in the coverage we've got tells listeners which one. Trustee races already get a fraction of the attention council races do. Voters deserve to know whether they're choosing between one incumbent and thirteen challengers, or an open board.

00:15:21.653 --> 00:15:43.277
<v Riley>Then there's the layer that barely registers in local coverage at all — the Cariboo Regional District electoral area director races. Those are the seats for the areas ringing Williams Lake but outside the city. Area D covers McLeese Lake and Williams Lake North. Area E covers Dog Creek and South Lakeside. Area F covers 150 Mile House, Horsefly, and Likely.

00:15:43.807 --> 00:15:50.087
<v Casey>Real seats, real land-use and budget authority, and nothing we've got names a single candidate in any of the three.

00:15:50.658 --> 00:16:03.290
<v Riley>So we're not going to invent one. If you live in Area D, E, or F, you're voting this October on who controls your regional district's decisions, and this show cannot currently tell you who's on your ballot.

00:16:03.875 --> 00:16:19.371
<v Casey>An email to the Tribune, the Observer, or the Free Press asking them to run the CRD candidate list plainly — the same way they ran the city and school district lists — would fix that in an afternoon. Races nobody covers get decided by whoever happens to recognize one name on the ballot.

00:16:19.924 --> 00:16:37.236
<v Riley>And that's the answer to what's worth saving. Nobody nominates the quiet infrastructure — the food bank volunteers we just talked about, an area director nobody's profiled, a trustee seat that changed hands mid-term without a headline. It survives or it doesn't based entirely on who shows up.

00:16:37.862 --> 00:16:49.510
<v Casey>Turnout with four separate ballots in one sitting is its own filter. People arrive motivated to vote for mayor. Plenty stop before the trustee section, and fewer still know there's a regional district line below that.

00:16:50.065 --> 00:17:03.569
<v Riley>Testable, though. Elections BC or the local returning officer could report how many ballots were fully completed against how many voters stopped after the mayoral race. As far as we can tell, nobody publishes that breakdown locally.

00:17:04.124 --> 00:17:18.636
<v Casey>So ask for it. Any community group in the Cariboo can put that request to the CRD or Elections BC after October 17th — how many people who voted for mayor never reached the area director line. No grant application, no budget. A phone call and a follow-up.

00:17:19.153 --> 00:17:56.137
<v Riley>And if the drop-off number turns out to be large, that's an argument for the paper covering the bottom of the ballot first next time, not last. Five weeks is enough time to profile three area directors and fourteen trustee candidates — that's less work than one mayoral debate recap. If you've got a name for Area D, E, or F, or you know who won that 2025 trustee by-election — or you're one of the candidates in those unnamed races yourself — email us, feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a. We'd rather report the actual name than leave the gap sitting there, and we'd rather hear it from you than guess.

00:17:56.672 --> 00:18:11.752
<v Casey>I'll still bet the mayoral race gets ten times the coverage of the other three combined, and that the drop-off number, if anyone ever produces it, is uglier than any of us expect. But the ask costs nothing, so make it. Bring a second pen on the seventeenth — it's a long ballot.

00:18:12.341 --> 00:18:16.837
<v Riley>All four sections, right to the bottom of the page. Have a good weekend, Cariboo.

00:18:17.017 --> 00:18:17.769
<v Casey>See you tomorrow.